. 
. * ============================
. * Analysis
. * ============================
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4
. 
.         * For consistency of approach between all different hypotheses and gi
> ven data
.         * availability of outcome variables (in particular of gender attitude
> s for H1
.         * and H4), we use a cross-sectional approach (OLS w/fixed effects) ex
> ploiting the
.         * random assignment of gender of the first child, conditional on beco
> ming a parent.
.         * Hence, we prioritize causality over perfect adherence to the hypoth
> esis, and we
.         * test the effect of having a daughter instead of a son as a first ch
> ild.
.         * See the PAP for a further discussion on the empirical approach.
.         
.         * Given the - to our understanding - limited availability of measurem
> ents of 
.         * gender attitudes in the SOEP-Core data, we use the variables that s
> eem to capture
.         * most closely two of the dimensions we are interested in: attitudes 
> towards 
.         * equality between genders in work-family balance (plh0308_v2) and at
> titudes 
.         * towards mothers who stay in the workforce (plh0298_v2 and plh0302_v
> 2). To 
.         * obtain only one coefficient, we create a standardized index as in A
> nderson (2008)
.         * out of the three variables. In the robustness checks, we also test 
> the hypothesis
.         * using each variable singularly.
.         
.         * In our main specification, we only include pre-treatment controls
.         * (i.e., sex of the parent and years since the first child was born +
>  FEs for 
.         * birth region and age of the parent and survey year).
.         * Accordingly, we run a linear OLS regression analysis with fixed eff
> ects with the
.         * gender attitudes index variable (generated using plh0298_v2, plh030
> 2_v2, and plh0308_v2),
.         * the dummy variable first_child_daughter as independent variable, an
> d the interaction 
.         * term between the treatment and the sex of the respondent (parent) t
> o then compute
.         * the difference in how much having a daughter affects gender attitud
> es of fathers
.         * and mothers. Direction-wise, if fathers' gender attitudes change to
> wards more equality
.         * is larger than the one of mothers, H4 is not rejected.
.         * See the PAP for a further discussion on the empirical approach.
. 
.         * Model 1: Basic specification with interaction
.         reghdfe gender_attitudes first_child_daughter male daughter_x_male, a
